
## V14 addendum: two acceptance defects found by re-reading the criterion, not by a failing test

Follow-up commits `0851f55` and `69dbb2d`. Both defects were in code that was already
committed and green, which is why they are worth recording.

### 1. The gate passed a run that beat nothing (`0851f55`)

The criterion is that the e2e run scores *above* the baseline. The gate asserted only
`regressed === 0`, and `regressed === 0` implies `delta >= 0`, so a run that improved
nothing at all would have reported success - the exact outcome the gate exists to
catch. The old test even pinned it (`assert.equal(result.passed, result.notWorse)`).

`passed` now also requires at least one fixture to improve, factored into an exported
`decidePass` so the rule is unit-testable rather than buried in the aggregation. The
mean delta is computed from the raw totals rather than from the two rounded means, so
the printed figure cannot disagree with the decision by a rounding step. Four new
tests cover the pass rule directly. The gate still passes on the real eval set
(delta +0.4, improved 2/12, regressed 0), now for the right reason.

### 2. The router invented a provider shape and duplicated the coding router (`69dbb2d`)

`src/models/route.ts` defined its own `UiModelProvider` with `hasKey`, `cooldownUntil`
and `strength`, plus its own availability rule. The real `FailoverProvider` carries
`kind` and `apiKeyEnv` instead, and `src/llm/codingRouter.ts` already owned chain
resolution and usability. So the two rules could disagree, and the specific
disagreement was harmful: a keyless cloud provider reported no `hasKey === false`, so
it looked available and would have been routed to, producing the failed request the
module's own doc comment claimed to prevent.

`routeUiModel` now reads the real `FailoverProvider` and reuses `coderChainIds`,
`usableProviders` and `codingProviders`. Cooldowns go through the real
`CooldownStore`/`providerKey` instead of a timestamp field nothing populated. The
reported chain is the full set of providers that can serve the chosen model, so
failover keeps its remaining candidates. `PipelineConfig` carries the real provider
type, the environment for the key lookup, and the cooldown store.

A parity suite now runs the same config through `routeUiModel` and `selectCoder` and
asserts they agree, so the two cannot drift again. Two new tests exercise the keyless
cloud case directly, one at the router and one through the whole pipeline.

### Gate results after both fixes

| Gate | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | exit 0 |
| `npm run build:server` | exit 0 |
| `cd packages/web && npx next build` | exit 0 (25/25 pages) |
| `npm run check:encoding` | OK |
| design + routing + gate + llm suites | 325 tests, 325 pass, 0 fail |
| `npm run check:design-pipeline` | delta +0.4, improved 2/12, regressed 0, exit 0 |

### Honest note on what the acceptance margin is

The mean delta is +0.4 across 12 fixtures, driven by 2 fixtures (the RTL dashboard at
+4 and the landing page at +1); the other 10 are level. That clears the criterion, and
the gate now fails if it ever does not, but the margin is thin and the task's own V1
eval set is what bounds it. Recorded here so the number is not mistaken for a wide
win.

