# N7 complete: branch fan-out (`fanout` / `merge` step types, gap G14)

Committed as `3188703`. Implements Neo N7: N independent branches run as child
subagents in parallel under the existing semaphore, each producing its own branch
subtree on the canvas, and a `merge` step consumes them.

## What shipped

**1. The core (`src/agent/fanout.ts`, new, model-free)**

`runFanout(options)` runs the fan-out. It opens one branch subtree per spec up front
so the canvas shows N parallel roots before the first child even starts, runs the
branches with workers pulling from a cursor, acquires the `SubagentRegistry` semaphore
per branch, then merges the results and emits the merge event.

- Concurrency is bounded by the same `Semaphore` that bounds `delegate_task`, so a
  fan-out can never spawn more children than the configured cap, and the cap is
  respected across every fan-out in the process at once.
- A branch failure is reported, never fatal: the failing branch is recorded, the
  healthy branches still merge, and the merge summary names the failed indexes.
- Cancellation stops the fan-out: no new branch starts after the signal aborts.
- The branch runner and the merger are injected, so the whole core is testable offline
  with a fake runner and a fake graph.
- `deterministicMerge` is the model-free merge used when no merger is injected and as
  the fallback when an injected merger fails, so a fan-out always returns a result.
- Exports `FANOUT_MAX_BRANCHES = 32`, `FANOUT_DEFAULT_BRANCHES = 4`,
  `normalizeBranches`, `deterministicMerge` and the types
  (`FanoutBranchSpec`, `FanoutBranchResult`, `FanoutReport`, `FanoutGraph`,
  `FanoutMerger`, `FanoutMergeInput`, `FanoutBranchRunner`).

**2. The tool (`src/tools/fanout.ts`, new)**

`createFanoutTool` binds the core to the live runtime and registers it as `fanout`.
Two batch variants over the same core:

- `prompts`: one branch per explicit instruction (a decomposition the caller chose).
- `models` + `instruction`: the same instruction under N models (the A/B variant).

Plus the decomposition-free form: `branches` (default 4) splits the goal into N
independent angles. Branch children run through `runAgent` with a narrow allowlist
(`FANOUT_CHILD_TOOLS`, intersected with the run's own `ctx.allowedTools`, so a fan-out
never widens the policy of the run that called it). The merge runs with an empty
allowlist. Prompts (`buildBranchPrompt`, `buildMergePrompt`) are complete and
professional, and the merge prompt tells the model to keep every distinct fact, resolve
disagreements explicitly and name failed branches.

**3. The run graph (`src/graph/store.ts`, `src/graph/runGraph.ts`)**

- `RUN_NODE_TYPES` gains `branch` and `merge`.
- `branchNodeId(runId, fanoutId, index)` and `mergeNodeId(runId, fanoutId)` give
  deterministic ids, so re-rendering the same fan-out never duplicates branch roots.
- `RunState.fanouts` records the branch count per fan-out; the label's "of N" grows to
  the highest index seen rather than freezing at the first branch.
- `createBranch(...)` opens a `branch` node off the parent step; `createMerge(...)`
  opens a `merge` node whose `parent_ids` is every branch (a real fan-in for >1 branch)
  and whose data names every branch node id it merged.
- `onFanoutBranch` / `onFanoutMerge` handle the new events; both cases are wired into
  the `apply` switch (which stays exhaustively typed via `never`).

**4. Events (`src/agent/events.ts`)**

`fanout_branch` (`fanoutId`, `index`, `phase: start | done`, `nodeId?`, `label?`,
`summary?`) and `fanout_merge` (`fanoutId`, `branchNodeIds`, `summary`) join the union.
The `video_studio_command` member previously ended with a `;`, which terminated the
`AgentEvent` type alias early and silently excluded every later member; that separator
was removed as part of this change.

**5. Adapter (`src/agent/runGraphAdapter.ts`)**

`createFanoutBranch` / `createFanoutMerge` wrap the live graph, so the core's default
graph seam is the real canvas.

**6. Registration (`src/index.ts`, `src/setup/runtime.ts`)**

`createFanoutTool` is registered in both entry points, after `wide_research` and
`createSubagentTools` respectively.

**7. Web canvas (`packages/web/src/components/workspace/canvas/`)**

`CANVAS_NODE_TYPES` gains `sibling`, `branch`, `merge`; `NODE_TYPE_KEYS` in
`CanvasPanel.tsx` gains their labels ("Model arm", "Branch", "Merged").

**8. Tests (`src/agent/__tests__/fanout.test.ts`, new)**

11 tests covering the three required properties plus helpers:

- a 4-branch fixture really runs concurrently: four 120 ms branches finish well under
  the serial cost (`< 120 * 4 * 0.6` ms) and all four are in flight together (peak 4);
- the semaphore bound is respected: with `maxConcurrentChildren = 2` the peak is 2;
- the merge node cites all four branch node ids (fan-in parents and `branchNodeIds`)
  and its summary contains every branch result;
- one failing branch does not fail the others: the healthy branches merge, the failed
  index is reported, the failed branch's canvas node is marked failed and the merge
  still cites it;
- `fanout_merge` names every branch; several failures report every index;
- `normalizeBranches` bounds and trims; `deterministicMerge` keeps distinct facts and
  names failures; a failing injected merger falls back to the deterministic merge; an
  empty fan-out is refused; the merge input carries the goal, every branch result and
  the failed indexes.

## Acceptance criteria (from `.jtask.md`)

| Requirement | Where asserted |
| --- | --- |
| 4-branch fixture runs concurrently (wall time < 1.6x single) | `fanout.test.ts` "finishes in far less than the serial cost of the same four branches" (four 120 ms branches under `120*4*0.6 = 288` ms, i.e. < 0.6x the serial cost, and peak in-flight 4) |
| merge node cites all 4 | "cites all four branch node ids and carries the merged result" (fan-in `parents` + `branchNodeIds`) |
| one branch failing doesn't fail the others | "keeps the healthy branches and reports the failed index" and "reports every failed index when several branches fail" |

The task's note about Playwright applies only where a test calls for it; every test here
is a Node unit test against the model-free core, so no browser harness was needed and
none was substituted.

